### GATE M FAILED ITS OWN BAR, AND IS RECORDED HERE AS AN ACCEPTED EXCEPTION
### AWAITING THE USER'S RATIFICATION.  (2026-09-22, review of the earlier wording)

This section previously read "eleven of twelve within the bar". By the letter of the note's
gate M, the gate FAILED, on two counts, and saying so is the point of having a bar:

  1. The ±0.3 pp movement bar: g=16 n=4 moved +0.502 pp. That is one mesh outside, not
     "eleven within".
  2. The "≤ 1.5 % absolute" bar: g=16 n=3 (15.37 %) and g=16 n=4 (3.80 % BASE / 4.30 % AFTER)
     violate it in BOTH arms.

Why it is accepted anyway, and what would overturn that:

  (1) The ±0.3 pp bar is a bar on a DIFFERENCE, and the differences are not systematic. The
      g=8 block moves both ways — -0.283, -0.179, -0.070, +0.050, +0.053, 0.000 — i.e. a spread
      of 0.34 pp with no sign, which is the size of the ghost scheme's OWN policy bias (the M2a
      study's 0.2-0.3 %, the number the note's own calibration criterion is written against). A
      change that moved the policy error systematically would not alternate in sign across six
      meshes of one family. g=16 n=4's +0.502 pp is the largest single excursion and it is
      +0.5 pp on a mesh whose absolute error is 4 %: a 12 % relative change in a quantity that
      is already ten times the calibration target.
  (2) The "≤ 1.5 % absolute" bar is MIS-SPECIFIED for this configuration, not violated by this
      change. Its stated reference is "0.1-1.3 % (M2a)", which is the M2a table's policy error
      on the DILUTE Z&H sphere family — a geometry with no throat, where the gap floor is inert
      (amr_two_sphere_gap.py's own docstring says so: "the Phase-2 Z&H ladder could not
      calibrate it because a dilute sphere array has no throats"). The two-sphere throat case
      exists precisely because that family cannot calibrate n. g=16 n=3 at 15.4 % is the gap
      floor pinching a 16-cell throat down to 3 cells across; that is a RESULT of the policy
      sweep (the script's docstring: "the aggressive end can pinch the throat outright ... that
      is the failure the floor exists to prevent, so it is a RESULT, not a bug"), and it is
      IDENTICAL in both arms to four decimal places. Importing a dilute-array tolerance into a
      throat sweep was an error in writing the gate, and it is flagged here rather than quietly
      passed.
  (3) Nothing in either count is caused by the per-FACE gate in the direction the gate could
      cause it. Every mesh whose census is 0 is bit-identical between the arms (rows g=8 ctl/n=1
      and g=16 ctl/n=1/n=2 — Δ exactly 0.000), and the largest movers are not the largest
      censuses (g=8 n=6 has 3328 withheld slots and moves -0.070 pp; g=16 n=4 has 576 and moves
      +0.502 pp), so the movement is not monotone in the amount withheld either.

  NOT RATIFIED. Re-writing a gate's bar after seeing the measurement is exactly the move the
  decision register exists to prevent, so this stands as an OPEN item for the user: either
  (a) accept gate M as passed with the exception above recorded, or (b) re-specify gate M's
  bars against the throat configuration (a movement bar of ±0.6 pp would pass as measured; the
  absolute bar should be dropped or restated per-mesh) and re-run. Nothing downstream depends on
  the answer; the per-FACE gate's correctness rests on rule (I) and the byte gate, not on M.

### THE CENSUS ANSWERS THE NOTE'S OPEN QUESTION 5, AS A MEASUREMENT

Open question 5 (build option C, sub-face closures, later?) asks for the census paired with
gate M. It is the cfCutFaces column above, and it converts the earlier inference into a fact:

  * Every mesh with cfCutFaces = 0 has Δ = 0.000 pp EXACTLY (g=8 ctl/1; g=16 ctl/1/2). The
    claim "the meshes whose k did not move are the ones where the gate withholds nothing" is
    now measured, not inferred, and it holds in both directions on this family: no mesh with a
    zero census moved, and every mesh that moved has a non-zero census.
  * g=16 n=3 has cfCutFaces = 256 and Δ = 0.000 pp to four decimals. So a non-zero census does
    NOT imply a measurable move: 256 withheld slots out of 5216 leaves is below this
    observable's resolution.
  * The withheld set does not grow with mesh size in any simple way (g=8: 0, 384, 1024, 1328,
    3328, 2616 over 4k-77k leaves), because it is the intersection of a level boundary with the
    cut band — a CURVE, as §7 of the note argues, not a surface. The largest census, g=8 n=6 at
    3328 slots = 1664 sub-faces on 43632 leaves, is 3.8 % of the leaf count, and that mesh's
    policy error moved by -0.070 pp.
  * On the note's own criterion for option C (revisit only if the policy error moves by MORE
    than the ghost scheme's 0.2-0.3 % own bias), the g=8 block's -0.283...+0.053 pp and g=16's
    0.000...+0.502 pp straddle it. DEFER stands, with the g=16 n=4 excursion as the datum to
    re-check if option C is ever reconsidered.


